  "stemBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Simplify (x⁴ × 12x³) / (2x² × 4x⁵)."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Think first: Simplify the top and the bottom separately before you compare them."
    },
    {
      "type": "asset",
      "storagePath": "assets/question_visuals/p065_q22.jpg",
      "altText": "Source visual for edmar-csec-p065-q22 from workbook page 65."
    }
  ],
  "solutionSteps": [
    {
      "stepNo": 1,
      "instruction": "Simplify the numerator.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "1 × 12 = 12 and x⁴ × x³ = x^(4+3)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: 12x⁷"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 2,
      "instruction": "Simplify the denominator.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "2 × 4 = 8 and x² × x⁵ = x^(2+5)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: 8x⁷"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 3,
      "instruction": "Subtract the indices.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "x⁷ ÷ x⁷ = x^(7−7) = x⁰ = 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: 1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 4,
      "instruction": "Simplify the number fraction.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "12/8, dividing both by 4"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: 3/2"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "strategyBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Collapse the numerator and the denominator into single terms, then divide coefficients and subtract indices."
    }
  ],
  "finalAnswerBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "(x⁴ × 12x³) / (2x² × 4x⁵) = 3/2"
    }
  ],
  "whyThisWorks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "When the same power appears on the top and the bottom, subtracting the indices gives x⁰, and any non-zero base raised to the power zero equals 1. That is why the letters vanish completely and only the number fraction survives. The zero index is not a special case invented for convenience; it follows directly from the division law."
    }
  ],
